# Timing & Performance

SvelteDiff computes whenever `originalText`, `modifiedText`, `diffMode`, `timeout`, or a cleanup option changes. The `onProcessing` callback exposes the cost of that work.

## Compact equal-text DOM

`compact` defaults to `true`. Unstyled built-in equal segments render as text instead of
wrapper spans, reducing DOM weight without changing text content or intrinsic line breaks when no
selectors or styles depend on the legacy wrapper. Custom equal snippets, `renderers.equal`, and
`rendererClasses.equal` retain their requested markup.

Use the legacy DOM only when existing selectors or styles require equal spans:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import { SvelteDiff } from '@humanspeak/svelte-diff'
</script>

<SvelteDiff
    originalText="The old text"
    modifiedText="The new text"
    compact={false}
/>
```

## Measure a diff

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import type { SvelteDiffTiming } from '@humanspeak/svelte-diff'

    let timing = $state<SvelteDiffTiming>({ main: 0, cleanup: 0, total: 0 })
</script>

<SvelteDiff
    {originalText}
    {modifiedText}
    onProcessing={(nextTiming) => (timing = nextTiming)}
/>

<dl>
    <dt>Core algorithm</dt><dd>{timing.main.toFixed(2)} ms</dd>
    <dt>Cleanup</dt><dd>{timing.cleanup.toFixed(2)} ms</dd>
    <dt>Total</dt><dd>{timing.total.toFixed(2)} ms</dd>
</dl>
```

## Timeout

`timeout` is measured in seconds and maps to the underlying diff-match-patch timeout.

```svelte
<SvelteDiff {originalText} {modifiedText} timeout={0.5} />
```

The default is one second. Use `0` for no time limit. An unlimited timeout can be appropriate for controlled offline inputs, but is a poor default for arbitrary user content on the main thread.

The algorithm returns the best diff it has when the deadline is reached; timeout is not reported as an exception.

## Reactive input guidance

For large editor documents, debounce text input before updating the values passed to SvelteDiff. This keeps typing responsive and avoids recomputing intermediate states the reader never sees.

```typescript
let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>

const scheduleDiff = (value: string) => {
    clearTimeout(timer)
    timer = setTimeout(() => {
        modifiedText = value
    }, 200)
}
```

## Cleanup cost

`timing.cleanup` includes whichever character-mode cleanup pass was selected; it is exactly zero for word/line. Semantic cleanup generally does more readability work than efficiency cleanup. Measure with representative data instead of assuming the faster choice.

## Scope

The component compares characters (default), words, or lines and renders the result. It does not virtualize large output, move computation to a worker, or expose incremental diff computation. For extremely large documents, consider a specialized editor diff engine.

Use the [timing example](/examples/timing) to edit inputs and watch the callback values update.

## Token work and deadlines

For word/line, `main` includes lossless tokenization, encoding, diffing, and decoding. `total` covers the timed computation. Expected-pattern preprocessing and DOM rendering are excluded. One absolute deadline covers all token stages, checked periodically during preparation and decoding. Expiry may return a full delete/insert; more than 65,535 distinct tokens shared across the inputs uses the same coarse fallback. Neither fallback truncates text or switches to character boundaries.

This seconds-based timeout is best effort, not a hard cap on regex execution, one large token allocation, or rendering. Token modes remain synchronous and do not offload, virtualize, or debounce work. SSR computes initial markup. Changing only `onProcessing` preserves the tuple array identity. See [Diff Modes](/docs/guides/diff-modes) for exact token rules.
